        "notes": "The claim that the LP $Gx\\ge0,\\,x\\ge0$ is 'always feasible' is trivially true since $x=0$ satisfies $G\\cdot0=0\\ge0$ for any G — so the cited witness ($T_e$, $y_{(k)}=0$) and indeed the whole 'always feasible' remark is vacuously true and does not distinguish stable from unstable atoms unless an additional normalization/non-triviality constraint (e.g. a fixed total demand) is imposed. As stated the homogeneous feasibility claim is true-but-degenerate, bordering on misleading. Same degeneracy threatens the ILP definition (x=0 is feasible)."
